==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|actual|ity}}.
* {{sense|television}} Influenced by {{der|en|fr|actualité}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌækt͡ʃuˈælɪti/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-actuality.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of [[existing]]; [[existence]].
# An instance or quality of being [[actual]] or [[factual]]; [[fact]].
# {{lb|en|television}} [[live|Live]] [[report]]ing on [[current affairs]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1964|author=European Broadcasting Union|journal=EBU Review: Programmes, Administration, Law|volume=83|page=22
|passage=A cabled despatch is better than nothing, but a voicecast of tolerable quality is preferable, and in certain types of story a voicecast with live or recorded '''actuality''' is best of all. Correspondents all have portable tape recorders.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Alan R. Stephenson; David E. Reese; Mary E. Beadle|title=Broadcast Announcing Worktext|page=164
|passage=This is a news report from the scene of the event. When a [[voicer]] and an '''actuality''' are combined into one complete story, it's known as a wrap.}}
# {{lb|en|historical|film}} A short early [[motion picture]].
#* {{quote-web
|en
|year=1999
|url=http://memory.loc.gov/ammem/vshtml/vsact.html
|title=The Variety Stage collection
|work=Selected Materials from the Library of Congress
|passage=By 1903, the '''actuality''' film had reached its peak; in 1903, the Edison and Biograph companies, combined, registered three hundred fifty one '''actuality''' films for copyright protection. By 1908 that number had dropped to two.}}

